From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 21:58:49 -0400
Subject: feat: little windows on Windows, and essentials that shrink to fit

Little windows on Windows (BACKLOG S-56; little E1, E2, E4; routing D3):
Windows hands a link from another app over as the address after
--single-argument, the way the installer registers Stedding for http and
https. With "Open links from other apps in a small window" on and no route
for the site, it opens in a little window, as on the Mac: while Stedding
runs, that is the whole launch, with no window of its own behind it; at
startup the window the session brings back comes first. Otherwise the link
is marked, so its tab lands in the Space for links from other apps, which
Windows did not do either. Settings shows the switch on Windows too, and
Ctrl+Shift+O in a little window sends its page into a split, as
Shift-Cmd-O does on the Mac.

Essentials that shrink to fit (sidebar Y33; BACKLOG S-276, GitHub issue
15): as in Arc, more cards share a row before the grid takes more rows: up
to four on one row, then two rows, the first the fuller, each card the
narrower, and a third row only once a card would be narrower than the
rail's square. They were two to a row whatever their number.

Tests: LittleWindowTest.ALinkFromAnotherAppOpensSmall (Windows);
SidebarRailTest.TheEssentialsShrinkToFitTwoRows, and
SidebarRailTest.TheHoverExpandedRailShowsTheEssentialsGrid for the new
row of three.

Why: links from other apps opened as plain tabs on Windows, and a long
essentials grid ran down the sidebar in rows of two (GitHub issue 15).
Removable when: never; both are product behaviour.
